<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Stambolov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Stambolov was a Bulgarian revolutionary and statesman. As regent after the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-of-battenberg">Alexander of Battenberg</a> in 1886, he secured the election of Ferdinand of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, and as prime minister from 1887 to 1894 he ruled firmly, resisting Russian influence and building up the state. He was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-stambolov.jpg" alt="Stefan Stambolov" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georgi Danchov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stefan_Stambolov_by_Georgi_Danchov_Zografina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stefan Nikolov Stambolov was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a>, the son of a man who had taken part in the Velchova Zavera plot against Turkish rule in 1835. He began his education in his home town and studied at the seminary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a> from 1870 to 1872. He joined the Bulgarian Revolutionary Central Committee, and after the death of its founder Vassil Levski he was chosen as his successor. He led the unsuccessful uprising at Stara Zagora in 1875 and the Turnovo revolutionary committee in the April Uprising of 1876, and after the Treaty of Berlin he was a leader of the Kresna-Razlog uprising of 1878-1879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Stambolov took part in the first Bulgarian parliament, the Founding Assembly of 1879, and after 1880 became vice-chairman and later chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>. In 1885 he helped bring about the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Eastern Rumelia. On 20 August 1886 officers aligned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> overthrew Prince Alexander. Stambolov led a counter-coup on 28 August that removed the provisional government and became regent, and Russian hostility barred Alexander's return; he abdicated on 8 September. At 32 Stambolov was at once a minister, president and regent for an absent monarch.</p><p>He found a successor in Ferdinand of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a>, proclaimed Knyaz of autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> on 7 July 1887 and crowned on 14 August 1887. Stambolov had first supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carol-i-of-romania">Carol I of Romania</a>, but Russian opposition forced Carol to refuse. Stambolov then served as prime minister for seven years, from 1887 to 1894, and strengthened the country's diplomacy, economy and state power. Critics called his methods dictatorial. In 1891 members of the Supreme Macedonian-Adrianople Committee tried to kill him and instead killed the finance minister Hristo Belchev. He later clashed with Ferdinand, whom he blocked from seizing extra authority, and by 1894 he resigned, which Ferdinand gladly accepted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In July 1895 four men ambushed Stambolov in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> on his way to a club near his home. They beat him badly, and he died of his injuries a few days later. The plot came from the Supreme Macedonian-Adrianople Committee, which blamed his support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and his repression of the Committee. He is considered one of the Founders of Modern Bulgaria and was sometimes called the Bulgarian Bismarck. He appeared on the Bulgarian 20 lev banknote, and Stambolov Crag on Livingston Island in Antarctica is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan Stambolov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russian anger forced Alexander's abdication in 1886, and Ferdinand of Saxe-Coburg was chosen in 1887, while premier <b>Stefan Stambolov</b> built a modern state and army.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bulgaria">Principality of Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Veliko Tarnovo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1880" aria-label="Veliko Tarnovo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5168 634 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stefan Stambolov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5618 904h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5618 904h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.08,25.63&amp;year=1880">What was here in 1880? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
